ue encodings: the cipher map framework, its papers, and the monograph that unifies them.</description><category>trapdoor computing</category><category>cipher maps</category><category>quantitative information flow</category><category>encrypted search</category><category>oblivious computation</category><category>information theory</category></item><item><title>Synthesis: Codecs as Structure</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Fri, 15 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/</guid><description>The series closes by restating the codes-as-priors thesis across all twelve instances and connecting the wire-format side to the Stepanov type-algebra side.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-synthesis-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>synthesis</category><category>algebra</category></item><item><title>Bits Follow Types</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-codecs-functors-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-codecs-functors-stepanov/</guid><description>Codecs are not ad-hoc bit formats. They are constructions on the algebraic structure of types.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-codecs-functors-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>codecs</category><category>information-theory</category><category>category-theory</category><category>functors</category></item><item><title>When Lists Become Bits</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/</link><pubDate>Thu, 23 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/</guid><description>Prefix-freeness is the property that lifts the free-monoid construction into bit space.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2026-05-prefix-free-stepanov/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>codecs</category><category>information-theory</category><category>category-theory</category><category>monoids</category></item><item><title>Information Theory, Inference, and Learning Algorithms</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/media/information-theory-inference-and-learning-algorithms/</link><pubDate>Wed, 17 Dec 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/media/information-theory-inference-and-learning-algorithms/</guid><description/><category>information-theory</category><category>bayesian</category><category>coding-theory</category></item><item><title>What I Learned by Analyzing My Own Research as Data</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-10-raison-detre-analysis/</link><pubDate>Wed, 15 Oct 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-10-raison-detre-analysis/</guid><description>I asked an AI to analyze 140+ repos and 50+ papers as a dataset. The unifying thesis it found: compositional abstractions for computing under ignorance.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-10-raison-detre-analysis/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>meta</category><category>research</category><category>philosophy</category><category>oblivious computing</category><category>self-analysis</category><category>information theory</category><category>s-risk</category><category>AI alignment</category><category>privacy</category><category>existential risk</category></item><item><title>Succinct Bit Vectors and Rank/Select</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-06-succinct-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 22 Jun 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-06-succinct-wire-formats/</guid><description>A bit vector with O(1) rank and O(log n) select using only n + o(n) bits of space. The auxiliary index is asymptotically negligible while enabling constant-time queries.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-06-succinct-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>data-structures</category><category>succinct</category><category>rank-select</category></item><item><title>Arithmetic Coding</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-arithmetic-coding-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 12 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-arithmetic-coding-wire-formats/</guid><description>Arithmetic coding closes the gap between Huffman&amp;#39;s per-symbol integer lengths and true entropy. A single number in the unit interval encodes an entire sequence; 32-bit integer arithmetic makes it practical.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-arithmetic-coding-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>entropy</category><category>arithmetic-coding</category><category>range-coding</category></item><item><title>All Induction Is the Same Induction</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-09-30-universal-bayes/</link><pubDate>Mon, 30 Sep 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-09-30-universal-bayes/</guid><description>Solomonoff induction, MDL, speed priors, and neural networks are all special cases of one Bayesian framework with four knobs.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-09-30-universal-bayes/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>machine-learning</category><category>solomonoff-induction</category><category>bayesian-inference</category><category>information-theory</category><category>philosophy</category></item><item><title>Huffman Coding</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-08-huffman-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 04 Aug 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-08-huffman-wire-formats/</guid><description>Given a finite distribution, Huffman&amp;#39;s algorithm builds the prefix-free code with minimum expected length. It is the first entropy-optimal code in this series.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-08-huffman-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>huffman</category><category>entropy-optimal</category></item><item><title>The Beautiful Deception: How 256 Bits Pretend to be Infinity</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-07-01-beautiful-deception/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jul 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-07-01-beautiful-deception/</guid><description>Cryptographic theory assumes random oracles with infinite output. We have 256 bits. This paper explores how we bridge that gap, and what it means that we can.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-07-01-beautiful-deception/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>cryptography</category><category>hash-functions</category><category>randomness</category><category>information-theory</category><category>kolmogorov-complexity</category></item><item><title>VByte / Varint</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-vbyte-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 25 Feb 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-vbyte-wire-formats/</guid><description>VByte trades bit-level precision for byte-alignment, and that trade wins in practice. Most production columnar databases and network protocols use VByte for integer encoding.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-vbyte-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>vbyte</category><category>varint</category><category>byte-aligned</category></item><item><title>Entropy Maps</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/entropy-map/</link><pubDate>Sun, 18 Feb 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>admin</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/entropy-map/</guid><description>Entropy maps use prefix-free hash codes to approximate functions without storing the domain, achieving information-theoretic space bounds with controllable error.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/entropy-map/featured.webp" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>entropy-map</category><category>rate-distortion</category><category>bernoulli-map</category><category>probabilistic-data-structures</category><category>hashing</category><category>information-theory</category><category>bernoulli-model</category></item><item><title>Perfect Hashing: Space Bounds, Entropy, and Cryptographic Security</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-01-crypto-perf-hash/</link><pubDate>Thu, 01 Feb 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-01-crypto-perf-hash/</guid><description>Space bounds, entropy requirements, and cryptographic security properties of perfect hash functions.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-02-01-crypto-perf-hash/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>cryptography</category><category>hash functions</category><category>information theory</category><category>space complexity</category><category>entropy</category><category>perfect hashing</category><category>bernoulli-model</category></item><item><title>Rice / Golomb</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-09-rice-golomb-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 17 Sep 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-09-rice-golomb-wire-formats/</guid><description>Rice and Golomb codes are parametric: a single parameter k (or m) tunes the code to a specific geometric distribution. Choosing k is choosing your prior precisely.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-09-rice-golomb-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>rice</category><category>golomb</category><category>geometric-distribution</category></item><item><title>Fibonacci Coding</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-04-fibonacci-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 23 Apr 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-04-fibonacci-wire-formats/</guid><description>Fibonacci coding uses Zeckendorf&amp;#39;s representation to produce self-synchronizing codewords. Every codeword ends in two consecutive ones; a single bit flip corrupts at most two codewords.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2023-04-fibonacci-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>fibonacci</category><category>zeckendorf</category></item><item><title>Elias Delta and Omega</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-11-elias-delta-omega-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 13 Nov 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-11-elias-delta-omega-wire-formats/</guid><description>Elias delta and omega extend Elias gamma by recursively encoding the length prefix. Each step yields shorter codewords for large integers at a small constant cost for small ones.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-11-elias-delta-omega-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>elias-delta</category><category>elias-omega</category></item><item><title>Unary and Elias Gamma</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-06-elias-gamma-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sun, 19 Jun 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-06-elias-gamma-wire-formats/</guid><description>Unary and Elias gamma are the two simplest universal codes. Unary encodes n in n bits; gamma in 2 log2(n)+1 bits. Each implies a different prior over the integers.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-06-elias-gamma-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>unary</category><category>elias-gamma</category></item><item><title>Universal Codes as Priors</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-01-priors-wire-formats/</link><pubDate>Sat, 15 Jan 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-01-priors-wire-formats/</guid><description>Every prefix-free code is a hypothesis about the source. The codeword lengths determine an implicit probability distribution; the code is optimal when that prior matches the true source.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2022-01-priors-wire-formats/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Information Theory</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>information-theory</category><category>coding-theory</category><category>prefix-free</category><category>universal-codes</category><category>Shannon</category></item></channel></rss>